+{"unit":"drama-aqa / live-theatre-review","checked_at":"2026-09-06T01:35:34Z","spec_used":"specs/aqa/drama-8261-8261.md","findings":[{"id":"F1","lesson":1,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"The lesson says that the production used in the exam must be a live performance meeting the 50-minute, cast, dialogue and production-value requirements.","truth":"Those requirements apply to the compulsory live performance experienced during the course. The exam response may refer to a digital recording or streamed production.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama specification 3.1.3, verified on AQA's live theatre production page and in the supplied specification.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F2","lesson":1,"field":"flashcard_questions","severity":"LOW","claim":"The flashcard says a peer performance is always something a student may not write about in the live theatre question.","truth":"Option 1 (recommended): restrict the peer rule to the qualifying live performance, while retaining the permission to use digital or streamed productions in the exam. Option 2: retain the simpler rule. The specification forbids a peer performance for the compulsory live experience but does not explicitly state whether a digital recording used in an answer may be peer-produced.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama specification 3.1.3, verified on AQA's live theatre production page.","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"},{"id":"F3","lesson":2,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"The lesson repeatedly presents vocal and physical skills as compulsory together in every extended performer answer, including every 8-mark or 32-mark answer.","truth":"Option 1: retain this as a useful heuristic because published performer questions may explicitly request both. Option 2 (recommended): soften the universal wording and tell students to follow the focus of the chosen question. The specification gives question choices and does not prescribe a single checklist for every performer response; changing the repeated rule would ripple through several fields.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama assessment components and the fetched June 2023 question paper.","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"},{"id":"F4","lesson":3,"field":"exam_tip_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"The lesson refers to an 8-mark designer question as a fixed exam format.","truth":"The specification does not allocate a universal 8-mark designer question. In AQA's published June 2023 paper, the 8-mark task was performer-led and the design choice was worth 20 marks. Refer generically to a designer question.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama assessment components and fetched June 2023 question paper.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F5","lesson":3,"field":"exam_tip_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"A 32-mark live theatre answer must combine performer analysis with at least one design discipline.","truth":"Students answer one question from a choice. The chosen focus may be performers, sound, set or costume, so the response should address the theatre-maker work named in that question.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama specification 4.2 and fetched June 2023 question paper.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F6","lesson":4,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"All top-band answers must use a fixed three-part evaluative formula and a particular linking word.","truth":"High-level live theatre responses need analysis, evaluation and precise support relevant to the chosen question. The mark scheme does not prescribe a three-part sentence formula or a mandatory word.","evidence":"Fetched AQA June 2023 mark scheme for the live theatre question.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F7","lesson":4,"field":"conclusion_html","severity":"LOW","claim":"Audience reaction proves that a theatrical choice communicated its intended meaning.","truth":"Audience reaction can support a student's evaluative inference about how a choice was received; it cannot prove intention or success on its own.","evidence":"Inference from the AQA live-theatre assessment focus on personal analysis and evaluation; fetched June 2023 mark scheme permits audience response as supporting material.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F8","lesson":4,"field":"exam_tip_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Every 32-mark answer requires a conclusion on the production's central meaning and an acknowledgement of weaker moments.","truth":"The required evaluation concerns the theatre-maker work named in the chosen question and should be supported by precise production examples. There is no prescribed conclusion, central-meaning requirement or mandatory limitation.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama specification 4.2 and fetched June 2023 live-theatre mark scheme.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F9","lesson":4,"field":"exam_tip_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"There is a fixed 12-mark 'analyse intentions' question.","truth":"The specification does not set that universal question type. In AQA's published June 2023 paper, the 12-mark set-text task focused on use of performance space and interaction. Give advice by question focus, not an invented fixed label.","evidence":"AQA GCSE Drama assessment components and fetched June 2023 question paper.","verdict":"FIX"}],"lessons_clean":[],"summary":"Checked all four lessons against the supplied specification and current AQA pages, with the published June 2023 paper and mark scheme used to verify question-format claims. Seven surgical fixes are supplied; two wider wording concerns are left for adjudication."}

