+[
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"content_html","find":"<p>The production you write about in the exam must be a live performance of at least 50 minutes, with at least two actors, dialogue and a range of production values. You may not write about your set play in the live theatre question.</p>","replace":"<p>Your course must include a live performance of at least 50 minutes, with at least two actors, dialogue and a range of production values. You may use a digital or streamed production in the exam. You may not write about your set play in the live theatre question.</p>","severity":"HIGH","note":"F1: The live-performance conditions apply to the course requirement; digital and streamed productions may be used in the exam."},
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"knowledge_checks","path":"[3].q","expect":"The production you write about in the live theatre review must be at least _____ minutes long, excluding intervals.","value":"To meet the course requirement, the live performance must be at least _____ minutes long, excluding intervals.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F1: The 50-minute condition applies to the compulsory live experience, not every production used in an exam response."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"exam_tip_html","find":"For the 8-mark designer question, you must specify a design discipline (lighting, sound, set or costume) and name concrete elements within it &mdash; lantern type, gobo, fabric, colour, fitting. Justify each choice with reference to the moment&rsquo;s meaning or the directorial intention. Generic descriptors like &ldquo;dark mood&rdquo; or &ldquo;period costume&rdquo; alone do not demonstrate design knowledge.","replace":"For a designer question, specify a design discipline and name concrete elements within it &mdash; lantern type, gobo, fabric, colour or fit. Justify each choice with reference to the moment&rsquo;s meaning or the intended effect. Generic descriptors like &ldquo;dark mood&rdquo; or &ldquo;period costume&rdquo; alone do not demonstrate design knowledge.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: The specification does not define an 8-mark designer question."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"exam_tip_html","find":"In the 32-mark live theatre review, you must address at least one design discipline alongside your performer analysis. The mark scheme rewards responses that show the <em>interaction</em> between performance and design &mdash; how a performer&rsquo;s vocal choice and a lighting state worked together at a specific moment to produce a combined effect on the audience.","replace":"In the 32-mark live theatre question, focus on the theatre-maker work named in the question. Some options focus on performers; others focus on one design discipline.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F5: The chosen live-theatre option determines whether the response concerns performers or a design area."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"content_html","find":"Evaluation always requires: a judgement + a reason + evidence. If any of the three parts is missing, it is either description (no judgement), assertion (no reason) or generalisation (no evidence). All three parts must appear in top-band answers.","replace":"A clear evaluation includes a judgement, a reason and supporting evidence. Use all three parts to make your evaluation convincing.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F6: This is a useful structure, not a prescribed top-band formula."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[5].q","expect":"What word must always follow an evaluative judgement to make it substantiated?","value":"How can you make an evaluative judgement substantiated?","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F6: No single linking word is compulsory."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[5].a","expect":"Because (or 'which suggests'); the reason must always be stated.","value":"Give a clear reason and support it with evidence from the production.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F6: The answer should teach the required reasoning without prescribing one word."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"conclusion_html","find":"Audience response is valid evidence: if the audience laughed, gasped or fell silent at a specific moment, that response is proof that a choice communicated its intended meaning &mdash; use it explicitly in your review.","replace":"Audience response can support your evaluation: if the audience laughed, gasped or fell silent at a specific moment, use it as evidence of how a choice was received in your review.","severity":"LOW","note":"F7: Audience reaction supports an evaluative inference; it is not proof of intention."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[4].definition","expect":"The collective reaction of the audience at a specific moment, such as silence, laughter or a gasp, used as evidence that a theatrical choice communicated its intended meaning.","value":"The collective reaction of the audience at a specific moment, such as silence, laughter or a gasp, used to support a judgement about how a theatrical choice was received.","severity":"LOW","note":"F7: Audience reaction is supporting evidence, not proof of intended meaning."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"exam_tip_html","find":"In the 32-mark live theatre review, the examiner looks specifically for a &ldquo;substantiated evaluative judgement.&rdquo; This means your conclusion or summative statement must do three things: state clearly whether the production succeeded in communicating its central meaning, give at least one specific reason drawn from a named moment, and acknowledge any areas where the communication was less successful or where you had a reservations about a choice.","replace":"In the 32-mark live theatre question, make a reasoned judgement about how successfully the theatre-makers named in the question communicated meaning. Support it with precise examples from the production.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F8: Evaluation must follow the chosen question's focus; no particular conclusion or limitation is compulsory."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"exam_tip_html","find":"For the 12-mark analyse intentions question, the analytical method is the same as evaluation but focused specifically on what the playwright or director intended the audience to think, feel or understand at a particular moment. Always name the intention, name the theatrical method used to achieve it, and judge whether the method succeeded.","replace":"For a question about performance space and interaction, name precise choices and explain how they communicate the required meaning.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: The specification does not establish a fixed 12-mark analyse-intentions question."}
+]
